window.__labLoaded({"id":"1.5","title":"Z-Scores","goal":"Use z-scores to compare Maya's Statistics and Biology marks, and standardise the lesson's five quiz scores.","question":"Maya scored 85 in Statistics and 78 in Biology. The raw marks cannot be compared, because the classes differ: Statistics averages 70 with a standard deviation of 10, and Biology averages 65 with a standard deviation of 6. In which exam did she do better compared with her own class?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Compare Maya's two exams","text":"A z-score is (score - class mean) divided by class SD: how many standard deviations above or below average. Raw marks of 85 and 78 cannot be compared, but z-scores can.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nz_stat = (85 - 70) / 10\nz_bio = (78 - 65) / 6\n\nprint(f'Statistics: z = {z_stat:.2f}')\nprint(f'Biology:    z = {z_bio:.2f}')\nprint('Maya did better, relative to her class, in', 'Biology' if z_bio > z_stat else 'Statistics')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, as np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; we use zscore and norm later.","Statistics z-score: (her mark minus the class mean) divided by the class SD, so how many SDs above average she is.","Biology z-score by the same recipe, with Biology's own mean (65) and SD (6).","Print the Statistics z-score, rounded to two decimals.","Print the Biology z-score.","Compare the two z-scores and print the subject where she stands further above her class."],"meaning":"The Statistics z-score is 1.50 (15 points above a class mean, with SD 10). The Biology z-score is 2.17 (13 points above, but the SD is only 6). So 78 in Biology is the stronger result, even though the raw mark is lower, because it sits 2.17 SDs above her class instead of 1.50.","out":"Statistics: z = 1.50\nBiology:    z = 2.17\nMaya did better, relative to her class, in Biology","figs":[]},{"title":"Standardise a whole data set","text":"stats.zscore does every value at once. It divides by the population SD unless you set ddof=1, which gives the lesson's sample-SD values (-0.86, 0.26 ...).","code":"score = np.array([72, 85, 90, 68, 95])\n\nz = stats.zscore(score, ddof=1)\nprint('z (ddof=1):', z.round(2))\nprint('z (default ddof=0):', stats.zscore(score).round(2))","explain":["Store the five quiz scores from the lesson in an array.","stats.zscore standardises every value at once. ddof=1 divides by the sample SD, the version the lesson uses.","Print the z-scores rounded to two decimals.","Print them again with the default ddof=0 (population SD) to see the difference."],"meaning":"With the sample SD (11.6) the five scores become -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. So the 95 is 1.12 SDs above the mean and the 68 is 1.21 below. The default ddof=0 divides by the smaller population SD (10.37), so each z-score is a little larger in size.","out":"z (ddof=1): [-0.86  0.26  0.69 -1.21  1.12]\nz (default ddof=0): [-0.96  0.29  0.77 -1.35  1.25]","figs":[]},{"title":"Mean 0 and SD 1","text":"Standardised data always have mean 0 and standard deviation 1. The shape and the ranking do not change, only the scale.","code":"print(f'mean of z = {abs(z.mean()):.2f}')\nprint(f'sd of z   = {z.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint('same order as the scores?', (np.argsort(z) == np.argsort(score)).all())","explain":["The mean of the z-scores. abs() avoids printing -0.00 caused by tiny rounding error.","The standard deviation of the z-scores, using the sample formula.","Check the ranking: argsort gives the order of the values, and .all() checks every position matches."],"meaning":"The standardised scores have a mean of 0.00 and an SD of 1.00 by construction. The ranking is unchanged (True): z-scores only change the scale, not who scored higher or lower.","out":"mean of z = 0.00\nsd of z   = 1.00\nsame order as the scores? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Back to raw scores and percentiles","text":"Reverse the formula with x = mean + z x SD. For bell-shaped data, norm.cdf turns a z-score into the share of values below it. Only trust that if the histogram is roughly bell-shaped.","code":"print('IQ with z = 2:', 100 + 2 * 15)\nprint(f'share below z = 1.5: {stats.norm.cdf(1.5):.3f}')\nprint(f'share below z = 2.17: {stats.norm.cdf(z_bio):.3f}')","explain":["Go backwards with raw value = mean + z x SD. For IQ scores the mean is 100 and the SD 15.","norm.cdf(1.5) gives the share of a bell curve lying below z = 1.5.","The same for Maya's Biology z-score, which turns it into a percentile."],"meaning":"A z of 2 on an IQ scale (mean 100, SD 15) is 130. If the marks are roughly bell-shaped, about 93.3% of students score below z = 1.5, and Maya's Biology z of 2.17 puts her above about 98.5% of her class. Those percentages are only trustworthy when the data are close to bell-shaped.","out":"IQ with z = 2: 130\nshare below z = 1.5: 0.933\nshare below z = 2.17: 0.985","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"score <- c(72, 85, 90, 68, 95)\nscale(score)[, 1]\n(85 - 70) / 10\n(78 - 65) / 6\npnorm(1.5)","explain":["Store the five quiz scores in a vector.","scale() standardises the scores using the sample SD. It returns a matrix, so [, 1] picks the plain column of values.","Maya's Statistics z-score: (85 - 70) / 10.","Maya's Biology z-score: (78 - 65) / 6.","pnorm(1.5) gives the share of a bell curve below z = 1.5."],"expect":"z for the five scores: -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21, 1.12; Maya z = 1.5 (Statistics) and 2.17 (Biology); pnorm(1.5) = 0.9332","read":"scale prints -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. The next two lines print 1.5 and 2.166667, Maya's z-scores, so Biology is stronger. pnorm(1.5) prints 0.9331928: about 93% of a bell-shaped class lies below that point.","note":"scale() divides by the sample SD, the same as ddof=1. It returns a matrix, so [, 1] pulls out the plain vector."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives, then tick 'Save standardized values as variables'","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n72  85  90  68  95\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=score\n  /SAVE.\nLIST VARIABLES=score Zscore.","explain":["Tell SPSS we type the data in the syntax, in free format, into one variable called score.","BEGIN DATA starts the data lines.","The five quiz scores, separated by spaces.","END DATA ends the data lines.","Descriptives is the command that can also standardise a variable.","/SAVE creates a new variable named Zscore holding each score's z-score.","List the scores next to their z-scores, so we can read them off."],"read":"The Descriptives table shows Mean 82.00 and Std. Deviation 11.597, the numbers used. The list shows each score beside its z-score: -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. Maya's two z-scores (1.5 and 2.17) are worked out by hand as in the Python.","note":"/SAVE adds a new variable named Zscore using the sample SD, so the values are -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. LIST prints the scores next to them."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput score\n72\n85\n90\n68\n95\nend\negen z = std(score)\nlist score z\ndisplay (85 - 70) / 10\ndisplay (78 - 65) / 6","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Start typing the data by hand into a variable called score.","First score, one value per row: 72.","Second score: 85.","Third score: 90.","Fourth score: 68.","Fifth score: 95.","end tells Stata that the typed data are finished.","egen z = std(score) creates a new variable z holding each score's z-score, using the sample SD.","List the scores beside their z-scores.","Maya's Statistics z-score, worked out as a sum.","Maya's Biology z-score, worked out the same way."],"read":"list shows each score beside its z-score: about -0.86, 0.26, 0.69, -1.21 and 1.12. The two display lines print 1.5 and 2.1666667, Maya's z-scores, so her Biology result is the stronger one.","note":"egen std() uses the sample SD (n - 1), so z matches the lesson. The two display lines are Maya's z-scores, 1.5 and 2.1667."},"conclusion":"Measured against her own classes, Maya did better in Biology (z = 2.17) than in Statistics (z = 1.50), even though the raw Biology mark is lower. A z-score counts how many SDs a value sits from its mean, puts different scales on one yardstick and keeps the ranking. Caution: turning a z-score into a percentage (98.5% for 2.17) is only trustworthy when the marks are roughly bell-shaped.","practice":{"scenario":"Dana took an entrance test with two parts. She scored 66 in Maths, where applicants average 52 with SD 8, and 71 in Writing, where they average 60 with SD 11. She also kept a log of her study hours over six weeks and wants them standardised.","data":"hours = [4, 7, 5, 9, 6, 12]; maths: score 66, mean 52, sd 8; writing: score 71, mean 60, sd 11","tasks":["Compute Dana's z-score in Maths and in Writing, and say which part is stronger.","Standardise the six study hours (sample SD).","Assuming a bell shape, find the share of applicants below her Maths z-score."],"hint":"z = (score - mean) / sd. For the list use stats.zscore with ddof=1, and stats.norm.cdf turns a z-score into a share.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nz_math = (66 - 52) / 8\nz_writing = (71 - 60) / 11\nprint(f'maths z = {z_math:.2f}, writing z = {z_writing:.2f}')\nhours = np.array([4, 7, 5, 9, 6, 12])\nz = stats.zscore(hours, ddof=1)\nprint('z of the six values:', z.round(2))\nprint(f'share below the maths z (bell shape assumed): {stats.norm.cdf(z_math):.3f}')","answer":"Maths z = 1.75 and Writing z = 1.00, so Maths is the stronger part, even though 71 is the higher raw mark. The study hours become -1.08, -0.06, -0.74, 0.63, -0.40 and 1.65, so the 12-hour week is the most unusual. Assuming a bell shape, about 96.0% of applicants scored below her Maths mark.","out":"maths z = 1.75, writing z = 1.00\nz of the six values: [-1.08 -0.06 -0.74  0.63 -0.4   1.65]\nshare below the maths z (bell shape assumed): 0.960","figs":[]}}});
